## Introduction
Nanocomposites with multiple compositions and complex architectures have received much attention in recent years.[1l A large variety of nanomaterials with different compositions (e.g., metal oxide,[2] noble metal,[3] layered double hydroxide,[4] carbonbased materials,[5] metal sulfide/ phosphide[6]) and architectures (e.g., core-shel,[7] yolk-shell,[8] multi-shelled hollow structures[9]) can be used as building blocks, their integration leads to multiple functions in one system. This strategy has provided advanced composite materials with improved performance than one single structure, and found applications in energy storage/conversion, adsorption, biosensors, catalysis and bio-medicine.[10]
Crystalline metal-organic frameworks (cMOFs) assembled from metal ions or clusters with organic ligands have widespread applications.[11] Along with the rapid development of cMOFs, amorphous MOFs (aMOFs), which maintain the basic building blocks but without long-range crystallinity, have emerged recently.[12] Compared to cMOFs, aMOFs exhibited higher conductivity, more defective sites and improved photoluminescence efficiency[12a] In previous reports, aMOFs were synthesized using high-temperature heating,[13] high-energy ball-milling,[14] pressurizing $(\sim1\mathsf{G P a})^{[15]}$ or electron-beam-induced amorphization procedures,[16] and their applications are focused mainly in adsorption, separation and drug delivery. However, there is no report of aMOF-based nanocomposites with complex composition and structures,which is expected to have superior performance in other applications such as electrocatalysis.
Herein, a simple strategy has been developed for the synthesis of an aMOF-dominated nanocomposite (aMOF-NC） with a nanosheet on a core-shell nanorod architecture,each building block having a different composition (Scheme 1). Fe-based cMOFs (cMIL-88B;MIL $\v{x}=$ Materiaux deInstitute Lavoisier/Materials)[17] with a nanorod morphology are used as the starting material (Scheme 1l, a). After treated in a 2- methylimidazole (2-MelM) solution to induce amorphization, aMIL-88B nanorods are generated (Scheme 1l, b). By further adding $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ into the reaction solution, aMOF-NC is obtained (Scheme 1l, c), including a Fe-rich Fe-Co-aMOF core $(\bullet)$ and a
