## Results and Discussion
The cMlL-88B precursor was synthesized by a hydrothermal method (see experimental part), showing a spindle-like nanorod morphology as evidenced by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) characterizations (Supporting Information, Figure S1). cMIL-88B nanorods show a smooth surface and average length of $\sim400\ \mathsf{n m}$ and width of $\sim90~\mathsf{n m}$ . After immersing cMIL-88B in 2-MelM solution for $10\mathrm{\min}$ , the morphology of aMIL-88B (Figure S2)has little change compared with cMlL-88B. Reaction further with $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ for $30{\textsf{s}}$ leads to aMOF-NC with a uniform spindle-like nanorod morphology and rough surfaces (Figure 1a). The particle size of aMOF-NC is ${\sim}500~\mathsf{n m}$ in length and ${\sim}230~\mathsf{n m}$ in width, obviously larger than cMlL-88B. At higher magnifications, the nanosheets on the outer layer of aMOF-NC are observed clearly (Figure 1b, 1c),which are predominantly oriented vertically on the nanorod surface and interconnected to each other with open pores. The thickness of nanosheet was estimated to be \~ 10 nm as shown in Figure 1c.
TEM imagesofaMOF-NCshow the core-shellnanorod@nanosheet structure (Figure 1d, e). The outer layer thickness formed by nanosheets is $\sim70\ \mathsf{n m}$ .The selected-area electron diffraction (SAED） pattern shows no visible diffraction rings, indicating the amorphous nature of aMOF-NC (Figure 1f). The high-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF STEM), energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) line scanning spectra and element (Fe, Co) mapping images of aMOF-NC were shown in Figure 1g. The density of iron is the highest in the core region of nanorod and decreases towards the edge, almost no iron is detected in the nanosheet shell. In contrast, cobalt has a lower density in the middle than in the edge of nanorod, and exists also in nanosheets on the outer surface of aMOF-NC. The EDX spot scan on the outer shell region further indicates that the nanosheets have only Co without detectable Fe elements (Figure S3). The total $\mathsf{F e/C o}$ ratio is determined to be ${\sim}0.62$ by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES). The ligand content of aMOF-NC was determined to be ${\sim}61.0\%$ based on the total organic carbon (TOC) of digested solution, indicating the existence of a high content of organic ligand in the aMOF-NC. Compared to the pristine cMlL-88 (ligand content of ${\sim}76.2\%$ ， about $80.0\%$ of ligand was remained in aMOF-NC.
To further characterize the complicated structure of aMOF-NC, the sample was embedded in epoxy resin and then cut into ultrathin slices by ultramicrotomy for electron microscopy analysis. When cutting perpendicular to the long axis of the nanorod (Figure 2a),STEM image shows the nanosheets on the outer layer (indicated by a white arrow) and an obvious core-shell structure with the inner core has a lower contrast (Figure 2b). The EDX line scanning spectra of Fe, Co, O, C and N elements are also shown in Figure 2b and Figure S4. The distribution profiles of Fe and Co are similar to Figure 1g with a lower Co content in core than shell. The distribution of O is similar to Co, while this trend is reversed for N and C elements (Figure S4). This indicates a higher content of carbon-rich ligand in core than shell, thus the metal content and contrast of the core region is lower than shell. The observations from EDX line scanning are directly seen in the element mapping images. Moreover, the conclusions are consistent from studies of ultrathin slices cut parallel to the nanorod long axis (Figure 2c, d). From EDX spot scan, the $\mathsf{F e/C o}$ atomic ratios are determined to be ${\sim}2{:}1$ in core and ${\sim}1{:}2$ in shell (Figure S5). Therefore, it is concluded that aMOF-NC exhibits a Fe, N, C-rich core and Co, Orich shell in the Fe-Co bimetallic core-shell-structured nanorod, with its outer surface covered by Co-based nanosheets.
To characterize the structural difference, the X-ray diffraction (XRD)patterns of cMIL-88B,aMIL-88B and aMOF-NC were recorded. As shown in Figure 3a, the diffraction peaks of cMIL88B are in perfect match with the simulated patterns (Cambridge Crystallographic Data Centre (CCDC) 647646), indicating high crystallinity. After treatment by 2-MelM, the crystalline peaks disappeared, suggesting amorphization of MlL-88B. After introducing ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and the growth of nanosheets on aMlL-88B, still no crystalline peaks can be found in aMOF-NC, consistent with the SAED observation. The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra of three samples are presented in Figure S6, showing the presence of aminoterephthalate (donated as ATA) retained in aMIL-88B and aMOF-NC.No peaks associated with 2-MelM were found. The bands at 1650 and $1150\ c m^{-1}$ represent the Fe-O vibrational mode in FeOOH and the peak at about $665\ c m^{-1}$ corresponds to Co-OH stretching vibrations, revealing the formation of FeOOH and $C o(O H)_{2}$ . The Raman characteristic peaks (Figure S7) corresponding to FeOOH and $C o(O H)_{2}$ are also observed in aMOF-NC, further confirming the generation of FeOOH and $C o(O H)_{2}$ . Meanwhile, the peak intensities of -OH group in aMIL-88B and aMOF-NC are obviously higher than MIL88B,suggesting that the lower content of ATA ligand in shell compared to core (Figure 2)is associated with increasing -OH contents during the amorphization and sheet growth process.
(d) high resolution XPS spectra of Co 2p of aMOF-NC and $C o(O H)_{2}$ nanosheets (IV).
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was carried out to further investigate the valence states of the samples (Figure S8). Fe,C, N and O elements were found in all three samples. Co signal is observed only in aMOF-NC. The contents of iron, carbon, oxide,nitrogen and cobalt calculated by XPS are illustrated in Table S1 and S2. The relatively lower contents of N and C and higher contents of O in aMOF-NC and aMIL-88B compared to cMIL-88B are in accordance with observations of element analysis and FTlR.High-resolution XPS spectra of Fe 2p,O 2p and Co 2p are presented in Figure 3b. The Fe 2p spectra of cMIL88B and aMIL-88B show two main peaks at 710.8 (Fe 2p3/2) and 724.2 eV (Fe 2p1/2) with one satellite peak at $715.5~\mathsf{e V}$ with relatively lower intensity, in accordance with literature reports.[18] Interestingly, the peak at 715.5 eV exhibits the highest intensity in aMOF-NC. Moreover, the Fe 2p1/2 peak of $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{a\mathsf{M O F-N C}_{\mathsf{s}}}$ negatively shifts ${\sim}1.2\ {\mathsf{e V}}$ and the Fe 2p3/2 peak positively shifts ${\sim}0.5$ eV compared to that of aMlL-88B. Previous reports demonstrated that the incorporation of Co into Fe-based cMOFs,[19] oxides[20] or hydroxide[21l caused the shift of binding energy of Fe 2p, similar to the observations in this study, which can be ascribed to the effect of Co on the electron density at the Fe center.
The O 1s spectrum of cMlL-88B can be fitted to two surface species (Figure 3c), the carboxylate $(531.8\:\Theta\vee)$ and Fe-O bonds $(530.7\:\mathrm{eV})$ .For aMIL-88B, two additional peaks attributed to -OH group (531.2 eV) and lattice oxygen of Fe-OOH $(529.4\ \mathsf{e V})^{[22]}$ are observed， indicating the generation of FeOOH during the amorphization process. This result agrees with FT-IR observations.For $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{a\mathsf{M O F-N C}_{\mathsf{s}}}$ ,a peak at ${\mathsf{529.7}}{\mathsf{e V}}$ assigned to CoO bond is deconvoluted. Compared with aMlL-88B, the Fe-O and FeOOH binding energy decreases by ${\sim}0.60$ and ${\sim}0.15$ eV, respectively, corresponding to the shift of Fe 2p. The result is consistent with previous reports,[20a, 21b] further indicating the electron interaction between Co and Fe. Meanwhile,the peak assigned to carboxylate group disappears, suggesting that the surface O-contained group is -OH dominant.
The $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}2\mathsf{p}$ spectrum of aMOF-NC (Figure 3d) possesses two major peaks of Co 2p3/2 and $\mathtt{C o}2\mathtt{p}1/2$ at 781.5 and $797.4~\mathsf{e V}$ respectively, with two satellite peaks at 785.2 and $802.8~\mathsf{e V}$ The spin-orbit splitting value between $\mathtt{C o}2\mathtt{p}3/2$ and Co 2p1/2 is ${\sim}15.9$ eV, indicating the formation of $C o(O H)_{2}$ [23]  To  support this conclusion， pure $C o(O H)_{2}$ nanosheets also with an amorphous nature were synthesized (Figure S9） and analyzed by XPS. Compared to pure $C o(O H)_{2}$ nanosheets, the Co 2p3/2 and Co $2{\mathsf{p}}1/2$ binding energies of aMOF-NC increase by ${\sim}0.9$ eV, corresponding to the negative shift of Fe 2p.[19,20a,21b] Collectively, the core-shell nanorod is consisted of bimetallic Fe-Co aMOF and the outer surface is coated by $C o(O H)_{2}$ nanosheets with a ligand content of\~ $61.0\%$ ， generating an aMOF-dominated nanocomposite with complex structure and compositions.
To understand the formation mechanism of aMOF-NC,the amorphization process of cMlL-88B was explored.XRD was used to monitor the change of crystalline structure as a function of reaction time ( ${10-60\mathrm{\min}})$ in the solution of 2-MelM, the pH of which was measured to be $\sim8.3$ with little change during the process.As shown in Figure 4a, peak shifts of cMlL-88B were observed at 2 min, which may be ascribed to the lattice shrinkage caused by the solvent exchange.[24] After $5\min$ ， the intensity of crystalline peaks was dramatically decreased.When the reaction time was longer than $10\min$ , all the crystalline peaks disappeared, resulting in an amorphous structure. The supernatants collected at different reaction times were analyzed by ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) spectroscopy (Figure 4b). With the reaction time prolonged from 2 to 10 min, the peak intensity at 327 nm assigned to ATA ions gradually increased, suggesting the gradual release of ATA from the framework of cMIL-88B (Figure 4b, S10). After $10\min$ , the released ligand experienced little increase. Meanwhile, the Fe contents in the solid samples were tested by ICP-OES, showing a continuous increase till 10 min before reaching a plateau (Figure S9). The FT-IR characterization further revealed the ligand removal process (Figure S11), as evidenced by the decreased peak intensity atributed to carboxyl group of ATA. The XPS spectra further support the time-dependent -OH replacement and ligand release (Figure S12, Table S2). SADE studies also revealed that cMlL-88B was converted to aMIL-88B (Figure S13), in accordance with the XRD results. The STEM and element mapping images (Figure S14) show the uniform distribution of Fe, N, C,O elements in aMlL-88B, confirming the existence of ATA (content of ${\sim}69.2\%$ from TOC measurement).
To demonstrate the crucial role of 2-MelM in the amorphization process, NaOH and ammonia solution with the same pH value to 2-MelM were chosen to react with cMlL-88B. After 10 min of reaction, the crystallinity of cMlL-88B was well maintained in two solutions (Figure S15a, 16a). The FTlR spectra further show that the ligand of MlL-88B is not removed from the framework (Figure S15b, c, Figure S16b, c). When only changing the solvent of 2- MelM system into methanol or ethanol, the amorphization phenomenon was not observed neither (Figure S17, 18). Therefore, the amorphization process should be induced by simultaneously applying 2-MelM and $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ . In addition, directly mixing cMIL-88B with $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ solution for $2h$ resulted in a very low $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ incorporation amount $(\sim0.5\%)$ as evidenced by XPS (Figure S19),far lower than that of aMOF-NC $(9.75\%)$ prepared at a shorter time, verifying the important role of amorphization treatment to prepare the final aMOF-NC. When increasing the reaction temperature of aMOF-NC to $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24~\mathsf{h}$ ，the ligand was totally removed, forming mixed Fe-Co hydroxides (Figure S20)with the rod-like structure dramatically damaged. Both the structure and composition of obtained Fe-Co hydroxides are different from aMOF-NC. This synthesis was similar to the conventional ligand replacement process,[25] through which the metal hydroxides were generated and the ligand of cMOFs was directly and almost completely replaced by -OH under relatively harsh conditions such as high temperature, high pH and long reaction time.
To gain insight into the underlying mechanism, first principle based density-functional theory (DFT） calculations (see the Supporting Information for details) were performed to study the transformation pathway of cMlL-88B with and without 2-MelM.As shown in Figure 4c and d, all Fe atoms sit in a six-coordinated $[\mathsf{F e O}_{6}]$ unit in cMIL-88B (State 1). The direct hydrolysis of cMIL88B without 2-MelM undergoes a partially hydrolyzed intermediate with a five-coordinated Fe center by breaking a FeO bond (State 2). This is a thermodynamic unfavorable process with a calculated reaction free energy change $(\Delta\mathsf{G})$ of 0.59 eV, consistent with our experimental observation that cMlL-88B was not converted to aMIL-88B without using 2-MelM (Figure S14,15). The calculation results also explain why in previous reports high concentration of alkali (2 M of KOH solution), high temperature $(>90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ and long reaction time $(>5\ \mathsf{h})$ were needed to trigger ligand displacement reaction in aqueous solutions.[25] In contrast, with 2-MelM, the five-coordinated Fe center can be stabilized by replacing the partially hydrolyzed ATA by two 2-MelM molecules (State 3). This process is energetically favorable (△G from 1 to 3 is $-0.79\mathsf{e V},$ . Finally, the hydrolysis of State 3 forms State 4,which is also thermodynamically favorable (△G of $-0.09\ \mathsf{e V},$ ，thus 2- MelM is not found in the final hydroxylated aMlL-88B.
From the DFT calculation and experimental observations, a ligand-competition amorphization mechanism is proposed to explain for the formation of aMOF-NC. The cMIL-88B is firstly converted into aMlL-88B through a two-step ligand displacement reaction ( $\quad1\to3\to4.$ ): the partial replacement of ATA (evidenced by
ATA release，Figure 4b,S11）by 2-MelM $(1\rightarrow3)$ and further hydrolysis of the metastable intermediate structure $(3\to4)$ to form aMIL-88B. Meanwhile,as a commonly-used organic ligand, the hydrolysis of 2-MelM( $\langle\mathsf{p K b}=6.15$ ）produces -OH，which also accelerates the reaction $1{\rightarrow}3$ ， supported by the observation that cMIL-88B was not converted to aMIL-88B without -OH. The formation of aMlL-88B creates abundant vacancy defects, thus the added $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ prefers to accumulate on the edge and defect sites[23, 26] of aMlL-88B. The formation of a core-shell Fe-Co bimetallic framework with Co-rich shell and Fe-rich core suggests that 2-MelM assisted ligand replacement occurs inward from the outer surface of cMIL-88B. The ultra-thin $C o(O H)_{2}$ nanosheets are grown on the external surface through the reaction between -OH and excessive $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ ions. Finally, aMOFs-NC with core-shellnanorod@nanosheet structure is obtained. Therefore, our proposed ligand-competition amorphization strategy is different from the conventional ligand replacement process.
To demonstrate the structural advantages of aMOF-NC, oxygen evolution reaction (OER), a core but bottleneck halfreaction of several electrochemical energy conversion and storage systems,[27] was used to evaluate the performance. Control samples including cMIL-88B, aMIL-88B, Fe-Co aMIL-88B with little $C o(O H)_{2}$ (the synthesis and characterization results were shown in supporting information, Figure S21-23), mixed FeCo hydroxides, $a{\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ nanosheets and commercial ${\mathsf{R u O}}_{2}$ catalyst were also tested for comparison.The polarization curves were recorded by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) in 0.1 M KOH solution by using a three-electrode system at a scan rate of 10 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . As can be seen in Figure 5b, c, pristine MlL-88B exhibits an ultra-large overpotential of $512~\mathsf{m V}$ at a current density of 10 $\mathsf{m A c m}^{-2}$ ，indicating a poor activity for OER. After amorphization, the overpotential of aMlL-88B is decreased to $473~\mathsf{m V}$ at $10\mathsf{m A}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , revealing a higher activity compared to MlL-88B. By further incorporating ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ，the activity of Fe-Co aMlL-88B is further improved with an overpotential of $350~\mathsf{m V}$ at $10\ m\mathsf{A}\ \mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ suggesting that the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ doping in Fe-based MOFs is beneficial for the OER. The designed aMOF-NC possesses obviously enhanced OER activity with an overpotential of $249\mathsf{m V}$ at $10\mathsf{m A}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , which is also lower than those of $a{\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ nanosheets (370 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}$ at $10\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ ）and even commercial ${\mathsf{R u O}}_{2}$ catalyst $(290~\mathsf{m V}$ at $10\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}.$ . The higher activity of aMOF-NC than mixed FeCo hydroxides prepared by the high temperature ligand replacement method further reveals the positive contribution of remained ligand during OER process,[28] indicating the superiority of proposed ligand-competition amorphization strategy. The corresponding Tafel slopes of the catalysts were further used to study the OER kinetics (Figure 5d). The tafel slope of aMOF-NC (39.5 mV/dec) is much lower than those of cMlL-88B (157.2 mV/dec)，aMIL-88B（ $(134.5~\mathsf{m V}/\mathsf{d e c})$ ，Fe-Co aMIL-88B (88.4 mV/dec),mixed Fe-Co hydroxides (81.7 mV/dec), $a{\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ nanosheets $(95.3\mathrm{\mV/dec})$ and ${\mathsf{R u O}}_{2}$ $(83.1\ \mathrm{\mV/dec})$ ， further verifying the excellent OER performance.
The OER Faradaic efficiency was also investigated by the RRDE technique (Figure S24).[19b, 29] A ring current of ${\sim}34.0~\upmu\mathsf{A}$ and a disk current of ${\sim}0.185\mathsf{m A}$ was detected, and the Faradaic efficiency was calculated to be $99.3\%$ (see details in supporting information), showing the highly selective oxidation of water into $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ . To consider the effect of specific surface area on the intrinsic activity, the LSV curves of different samples were normalized to BET surface area (Figure S25) and electrochemical surface areas (ECSA, Figure S26).[30] The results (Figure S27) still display the best OER activity of aMOF-NC, confirming the highest intrinsic activity. Moreover, the OER performances of aMOF-NC are comparable with the reported best MOF-based catalysts under the same conditions (Table S3).
To explore the reason for enhanced OER activity of $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{a\mathsf{M O F-N C}_{\mathsf{s}}}$ . the ECSA, generally proportional to the double-layer capacitances (Cdl) of the electrocatalysts, were investigated via cyclic voltammograms (CV) at different scan rates from $10\mathrm{mVs^{-}}$ 1 to $100~\mathsf{m V s^{-1}}$ (Figure S26). The Cdl was calculated by plotting △j=ja-jc at $1.40~\mathsf{V}$ vs RHE against the scan tare. As shown in Figure 5e, the values of Cdl of the samples are gradually increased with the trend of d $M_{1}L-88B<a M_{1}L-88B~a C o(O H)_{2}<F e-$ $C o\ a M l L-88\mathsf{B}<a M O F-N\mathsf{C}\mathsf{s}$ .Moreover, the Cdl of aMOF-NC is also obviously higher than that of mixed Fe-Co hydroxides, indicating that the existence of ligand can increase the electrochemical surface area. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was further performed to study the charge transport kinetics of the sample. As shown in Figure 5f, aMlL-88B shows lower charge transfer resistance (Rct) than cMlL-88B, revealing the amorphous framework can facilitate the charge transfer.[19b, 31] After $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ doping, the Rct of Fe-Co aMlL-88B is further decreased.[21b] The Rct of aMOF-NC is lower than Fe-Co aMlL-88B and mixed Fe-Co hydroxides but higher than $a{\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ nanosheets, suggesting that the $a{\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ nanosheets located on the outer layer and remained ligand of aMOF-NC contribute to the good charge transfer ability. The relatively low activity of $a{\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ may be caused by the single metal center and small ECSA.
To understand the intrinsic active sites,we further calculated the free energy diagram of the most optimal OER pathway on the hydroxylated aMlL-88B with and without Co substitution. After removal of one ATA linker, a water molecule can adsorb on the exposed the metal site (state 4 in Figure 4). During the OER, this water molecule is stepwise dehydrogenated to form the atomic oxygen. Then the atomic O reacts with an additional solvent water molecule and eventually form the molecular $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ . The free energy change of each OER step was calculated at the equilibrium potential $\mathsf{U}=1.23\mathsf{V}$ . The free energy diagram are shown in Figure S28. It is found that the second electron transfer step $\mathrm{\DeltaOH_{ad}\rightarrow}$ $\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{a d}^{+}}\mathsf{H}^{+}+\mathsf{e}^{-})$ is the rate determine step. With the substitution of 1/3 Fe of aMlL-88B by Co, the free energy change of this step can be reduced by $0.31~{\mathsf{e V}}$ (from 1.12 to 0.81 eV). Since the free energy change of each electron transfer step decreases linearly with the increase of potential U, the Co substituted aMOF shows better OER activity. Our results are consistent with the enhancement of OER activity in Fe-based catalysts in the presence of Co,[19a,20a,21l indicating the active sites are located at the $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o O}_{\mathsf{x}}$ center in aMOF part of aMOF-NC.
Based on the characterization and electrochemical results, the enhanced OER performance of aMOF-NC is ascribed to the 3D hierarchical structure, amorphous characteristic and complex compositions (Figure 5a). (1) The 3D hierarchical architecture can facilitate the diffusion of electrolytes and $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ molecules, enhancing the mass transfer. (2) The amorphous framework is beneficial to provide more active sites， facilitated charge transfer and increased ECSA. (3) The growth of $a{\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ nanosheets on the surface of aMOFs can also improve the charge transfer on the interface between the catalyst and electrolyte, accelerating the reaction kinetics. (4) According to previous reports,[28] the ligand may promote the charge transfer from oxygen-contained groups to Fe and Co inducing an increase in electron density of metal active sites (evidenced by the lower Rct than Fe-Co hydroxides). Moreover, the exposed carboxyl groups in ligand can also improve OER activity by enhancing the hydrophilicity and availability of OH-. (5) The abundant metal centers can supply a large amount of active sites. Moreover, the interaction between Fe and Co sites in a bimetallic system can adjust the electronic structure of the catalyst and thus improve the intrinsic activity of the catalytic center.
In addition to the outstanding activity, stability of the electrocatalysts is also critical for OER.The stability of aMOF-NC in alkaline solution was firstly examined by suspending samples in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ of KOH for $24\ h$ at 25 and $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , respectively. In the FTIR spectra of samples after treatment, the peaks corresponding to the ATA ligand are well kept (Figure S29),indicating the good stability of aMOF-NC. To further explore the stability of aMOF- ${\mathsf{N C}}_{\mathsf{s}}$ during OER process, successive CV with a scan rate of $10\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-}$ 1 was performed. The LSV curve of aMOF-NC shows no significant increase of overpotential after 500 cycles (Figure 5g), suggesting its robust catalytic activity. The long-term durability of aMOF-NC was further evaluated by the chronoamperometric measurements (Figure 5h).After $24\ h$ ，only ${\sim}2.3\%$ of current decay is observed in $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{a\mathsf{M O F-N C}_{\mathsf{s}}}$ ，while the current loss at $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}$ catalysts is high up to $53.2\%$ ，indicating the excellent OER stability. Furthermore, the sample after cycling test was characterized. TEM image shows the morphology of aMOF-NC was slightly changed (Figure S30a). Meanwhile, the typical peaks corresponding to ATA are still observed in the FT-lR spectra (Figure S31), indicating the maintenance of aMOF part. The XRD pattern (Figure S3ob) shows the generation of low crystaline $C o(O H)_{2}$ (JCPDS no. 02-0925). The XPS spectra of aMOF-NC (Figure S32) after use also displayed the similar electronic structures of Co and Fe centers compared to the fresh one. Therefore, the chemical composition of aMOF-NC and chemical structure of metal center are remained.
## Conclusion
In summary,aMOF- ${\mathsf{N C}}_{\mathsf{s}}$ with complex structure and composition was synthesized by a ligand-competition amorphization pathway. The core-shell-nanorod@nanosheet structured $a{\mathsf{M O F-N C}}_{\mathsf{s}}$ is consisted of a Fe-rich Fe-Co-aMOF core and a Co-rich Fe-CoaMOF shell in the core-shell structured nanorod, and $a{\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ nanosheets as the outer layer. The distinctive features including amorphous framework, 3D hierarchical architecture and abundant Fe-Co bimetal centers endow aMOF-NC with superior OER activity and stability. The active sites of aMOF-NC are further identified by DFT calculations. Our contribution has provided an efficient strategy for the synthesis of aMOF-based composites with enhanced performance.
